**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), coagulation of many sizes. Follow the later kernel and growth treatments in the complete notes.

Let n_k count clusters containing k identical material units. Under binary merging, dn_k/dt=(1/2)Σ_{i+j=k}β_ij n_i n_j−n_kΣ_jβ_kj n_j. The first term creates class k, while the second removes it when it meets any partner. The symmetric kernel and factor convention must be consistent.

For an untruncated nonfragmenting, nonreacting closed system, Σk n_k is the material-unit concentration and is conserved. Total number Σn_k decreases. A finite numerical size range can lose mass when products exceed its last class; hiding that loss by renormalizing the distribution conceals a model error.

**Work:** Derive both moment balances by relabelling the sums. Implement a small event-based example with an explicit overflow class and compare its material inventory with a truncated version. Distinguish exact conservation of a discretization from accuracy of its predicted distribution.

